SAT_CHUNK("rw/boundaries/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.acc0537684","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-0","passage":"<p>In her study of nineteenth-century textile mills, historian Mei Lin Zhou found that workers' letters home rarely mentioned wages ______ instead, they described friendships, church meetings, and the lectures that some mill towns offered in the evenings.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["or hours;","or hours,","or hours","or, hours;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Both parts are independent clauses (\"workers' letters... rarely mentioned wages or hours\" and \"they described friendships...\"). A semicolon can join them, and \"instead,\" with its comma, starts the second clause. A comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. The comma after \"or\" wrongly separates the parts of \"wages or hours.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.7860c48f6a","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-1","passage":"<p>The Voyager 1 spacecraft, launched in 1977 to study Jupiter and ______ became in 2012 the first human-made object to enter interstellar space.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Saturn","Saturn;","Saturn—","Saturn,"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The phrase \"launched in 1977 to study Jupiter and Saturn\" interrupts the subject and verb. It opens with a comma after \"Voyager 1 spacecraft,\" so it must close with a matching comma. Omitting the comma leaves it unclosed. A semicolon would separate the subject from its verb, and a dash doesn't match the opening comma."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.e36bb47f2f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-2","passage":"<p>Architect Norma Merrick Sklarek, who in 1954 became the first African American woman licensed as an architect in New York ______ went on to help design major projects, including a terminal at Los Angeles International Airport.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["State","State,","State;","State:"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The clause \"who in 1954 became... New York State\" is set off by a comma before \"who,\" so it needs a closing comma before the main verb \"went.\" Without it, the clause runs into the verb. A semicolon or colon would wrongly separate the subject \"Norma Merrick Sklarek\" from \"went.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.f38c9b771d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-3","passage":"<p>The researchers tested three kinds of soil ______ compost made from food waste; biochar, a charcoal-like material; and crushed volcanic rock, which releases minerals slowly as it weathers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["amendments, such as:","amendments,","amendments;","amendments:"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A colon introduces the list that explains \"three kinds of soil amendments,\" and the list's items are separated by semicolons because they contain internal commas. A semicolon before a list is incorrect. A comma is too weak before a list whose items use semicolons. \"such as:\" wrongly places a colon after \"such as\" and implies the list is incomplete."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.d6b7253903","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-4","passage":"<p>Many of the manuscripts that the monks copied had been damaged by damp and ______ in some cases, entire pages had crumbled away before they could be transcribed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["insects: and","insects;","insects","insects,"],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Many of the manuscripts... damaged by damp and insects\" and \"in some cases, entire pages had crumbled away...\" are both independent clauses, so a semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone is a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. A colon followed by \"and\" is not a standard way to join clauses."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.3bb85244de","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-5","passage":"<p>Economist Esther Duflo's research on poverty emphasizes small, carefully tested ______ distributing free bed nets to prevent malaria, for example, or offering families a bag of lentils as an incentive to vaccinate their children.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["interventions, these include","interventions:","interventions. Such as","interventions;"],"answer":"B","explanation":"What follows gives examples of the \"interventions\" and isn't an independent clause, so a colon correctly introduces it. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it. \"interventions, these include\" makes a comma splice. \"Such as\" after a period starts a fragment."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.7c0239af1b","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-6","passage":"<p>The Pueblo potter Maria Martinez ______ developed a distinctive technique for producing black-on-black pottery in the early twentieth century, and her work helped revive traditional ceramic arts in New Mexico.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["and her husband, Julian","and her husband, Julian,","and her husband Julian,","and, her husband, Julian,"],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Julian\" renames \"her husband\" as a nonessential appositive, since she had only one husband. It needs commas on both sides. Without the opening comma, or without the closing one, the appositive isn't properly set off. The comma after \"and\" wrongly splits the compound subject."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.955abbe29c","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-7","passage":"<p>When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, it was the longest suspension bridge in the ______ its steel-wire cables, a relatively new technology at the time, allowed it to span nearly 1,600 feet between its towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["world, and","world and,","world","world,"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Both sides of the blank are independent clauses. A comma followed by a coordinating conjunction (\"and\") joins them correctly. A comma alone makes a comma splice, and no punctuation makes a run-on. Putting the comma after \"and\" is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.0a776a990d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-8","passage":"<p>Scientists once believed that deep-sea hydrothermal vents were too hot and toxic to support ______ when researchers first explored the vents in 1977, however, they found dense communities of tube worms, clams, and bacteria.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["life;","life, which","life,","life"],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Scientists once believed... to support life\" and \"when researchers first explored the vents in 1977, however, they found...\" are both independent clauses, and \"however\" is not a conjunction, so a semicolon is needed. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. \"which\" leaves the rest of the sentence without a grammatical structure."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.c3a3d16933","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-9","passage":"<p>The composer Florence Price's Symphony in E Minor, which the Chicago Symphony Orchestra performed in ______ was the first symphony by a Black woman to be played by a major American orchestra.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["1933","1933—","1933,","1933;"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The nonessential clause \"which the Chicago Symphony Orchestra performed in 1933\" opens with a comma, so it must close with one before the verb \"was.\" Leaving the comma out runs the clause into the verb. A semicolon or dash doesn't match the opening comma."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.82155dfa2c","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-10","passage":"<p>Though they are often called \"living fossils,\" ______ have changed considerably over millions of years, according to recent genetic studies.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["coelacanths—deep-sea fish once thought to be extinct—","coelacanths—deep-sea fish once thought to be extinct,","coelacanths, deep-sea fish once thought to be extinct—","coelacanths deep-sea fish once thought to be extinct"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The appositive \"deep-sea fish once thought to be extinct\" must be set off by a matching pair of punctuation marks. Two dashes are correct. Mixing a comma and a dash is incorrect, and omitting punctuation leaves the appositive unmarked."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.ba3ebc67d2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-11","passage":"<p>The play's title comes from a line in a poem by Langston ______ \"What happens to a dream deferred?\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Hughes;","Hughes,:","Hughes that","Hughes:"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A colon correctly introduces the quoted line that specifies which line of the poem is meant. A semicolon can't introduce a quotation that isn't an independent clause. \"Hughes,:\" doubles the punctuation. \"Hughes that\" followed directly by the quotation doesn't form a grammatical clause."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.85e7822067","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-12","passage":"<p>Solar panels generate the most electricity when the sun is directly overhead, but in many regions demand for electricity peaks in the early ______ when people return home and turn on lights, appliances, and air conditioners.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["evening;","evening.","evening,","evening:"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"when people return home...\" is a dependent clause that adds information about \"the early evening,\" so a comma correctly introduces it. A semicolon or period would leave the \"when\" clause as a fragment. A colon is used after an independent clause to introduce an explanation or list, not before a dependent clause like this."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.5276f67a8f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-13","passage":"<p>The Library of Alexandria is often said to have been destroyed in a single catastrophic fire. Historians now believe its decline was ______ the result of reduced funding, political turmoil, and the gradual loss of scholars over several centuries.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["gradual","gradual, it was","gradual,","gradual;"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase \"the result of reduced funding...\" explains \"gradual\" and is correctly set off by a comma. With no punctuation, the phrase runs into \"gradual.\" A semicolon requires an independent clause after it. \"gradual, it was the result of...\" joins two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.f219e220ce","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-14","passage":"<p>Marine biologist Sylvia Earle has spent more than 7,000 hours ______ in 1979, she walked untethered on the seafloor off Hawaii at a depth of 381 meters, a record that still stands.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["underwater, and,","underwater,","underwater;","underwater"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Both parts are independent clauses. The semicolon is correct. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and \"and,\" with a comma after it is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.80d1b05a2c","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-15","passage":"<p>The findings, published in ______ suggest that honeybees can learn to associate specific colors with rewards after only a few visits to artificial flowers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["2023:","2023;","2023,","2023"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase \"published in 2023\" interrupts the subject \"The findings\" and the verb \"suggest.\" It opens with a comma, so it must close with one. No comma leaves it unclosed. A semicolon or colon would separate the subject from its verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.2fc4d38b7d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-16","passage":"<p>Some cities have experimented with \"participatory budgeting,\" a process in which ______ vote directly on how to spend a portion of the city's budget.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["residents, rather than elected officials","residents, rather than elected officials,","residents; rather than elected officials,","residents rather than elected officials,"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The interrupting phrase \"rather than elected officials\" needs commas on both sides. Omitting either comma is incorrect, and a semicolon wrongly breaks the clause \"in which residents... vote.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.492af69cf4","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-17","passage":"<p>The ______ spent years living with the Kalahari San people, documenting how they shared food and resolved conflicts without formal leaders.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["anthropologist, Lorna Marshall","anthropologist, Lorna Marshall,","anthropologist Lorna Marshall","anthropologist Lorna Marshall,"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Lorna Marshall\" is an essential appositive: it identifies which anthropologist, so no commas are used. A comma after \"Marshall\" wrongly separates the subject from the verb \"spent.\" The versions with commas before the name treat it as nonessential and incorrectly separate \"anthropologist\" from the name that specifies it."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.2542f9608a","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-18","passage":"<p>The study's authors acknowledge an important ______ because participants reported their own sleep habits, the data may be less accurate than measurements taken in a sleep laboratory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["limitation,","limitation:","limitation","limitation, which"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The second part explains what the limitation is, so a colon fits after the independent clause. A comma creates a comma splice, since both parts are independent clauses. No punctuation makes a run-on. \"which\" leaves the rest of the sentence ungrammatical."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.b4cfe6d8cc","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-19","passage":"<p>In the 1600s, Dutch merchants traded tulip bulbs at astonishing ______ single bulb of a rare variety reportedly sold for more than the price of a house in Amsterdam.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["prices a","prices, and, a","prices, a","prices; a"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Both parts are independent clauses. The semicolon correctly separates them. A comma alone is a comma splice, no punctuation is a run-on, and \"and,\" with commas around it is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.d298611297","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-20","passage":"<p>Ecologists refer to species like sea otters and wolves as keystone ______ their effects on an ecosystem are far greater than their numbers would suggest.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["species. Because","species; because","species, because,","species because"],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"because their effects...\" is a dependent clause that follows the main clause without a comma. The comma after \"because\" is incorrect. A semicolon or period would turn the dependent clause into a fragment."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.549c85e57e","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-21","passage":"<p>The Great Green Wall, an initiative to plant a band of trees and restore degraded land across the width of ______ has faced challenges, including drought, conflict, and inconsistent funding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Africa—","Africa,","Africa;","Africa"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The appositive beginning \"an initiative to plant...\" opens with a comma after \"The Great Green Wall,\" so it closes with a comma before \"has.\" A dash doesn't match the opening comma. No comma or a semicolon would incorrectly separate or join the subject and verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.0fe7ce78b6","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-22","passage":"<p>Astronomers study the light from distant stars to learn about their ______ the patterns of dark lines in a star's spectrum reveal which elements its atmosphere contains.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["composition,","composition:","composition, where","composition"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The second clause explains how the light reveals composition, and both parts are independent clauses. A colon correctly introduces that explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. \"where\" makes the sentence illogical, since the dark lines aren't located \"in\" the composition."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.5f8a10e25f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data5-23","passage":"<p>The Mexican painter ______ \"The Two Fridas,\" depicts two versions of the artist seated side by side, holding hands.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Frida Kahlo's, 1939 work,","Frida Kahlo's 1939 work;","Frida Kahlo's 1939 work,","Frida Kahlo's 1939 work"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"The Two Fridas\" renames the work as a nonessential appositive; since the sentence already opens the appositive with a comma after its title, the phrase before it must be followed by a comma: \"Frida Kahlo's 1939 work, 'The Two Fridas,' depicts.\" Omitting the comma leaves the appositive unbalanced. The comma after \"Kahlo's\" splits the possessive from its noun, and a semicolon breaks the subject from its verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.230998438e","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-0","passage":"<p>The oldest known map of the stars, painted on silk in China around 700 ______ shows more than 1,300 stars arranged in 257 constellations.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["CE,","CE","CE:","CE;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The phrase \"painted on silk in China around 700 CE\" interrupts the subject (\"The oldest known map of the stars\") and the verb (\"shows\"). It opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma. Without a comma the phrase runs into the verb, and a semicolon or colon would separate the subject from its verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.6f98f8ec57","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-1","passage":"<p>Beavers are sometimes called ecosystem ______ by building dams, they create ponds and wetlands that provide habitat for fish, birds, and amphibians.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["engineers,","engineers:","engineers","engineers, which"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The second part explains why beavers earn the name, and both parts are independent clauses, so a colon fits. A comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. \"which\" leaves \"by building dams, they create...\" without a place in the sentence's structure."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.dd30b6608e","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-2","passage":"<p>The team's findings challenge a long-held ______ the Maya abandoned their cities suddenly, perhaps after a single catastrophic drought.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["assumption that","assumption. That","assumption, that","assumption; that"],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"that the Maya abandoned their cities suddenly...\" is a clause that tells what the assumption is, and it is essential to the noun, so no punctuation separates \"assumption\" from \"that.\" A comma wrongly sets the clause off. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, and a period leaves \"That the Maya abandoned...\" as a fragment."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.2f797b3bab","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-3","passage":"<p>Some of the world's longest-lived trees are bristlecone pines, which grow in the White Mountains of ______ one of these trees, nicknamed Methuselah, is estimated to be more than 4,800 years old.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["California;","California,","California","California, and,"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence contains two independent clauses: \"Some of the world's longest-lived trees are bristlecone pines, which grow in the White Mountains of California\" and \"one of these trees... is estimated to be more than 4,800 years old.\" A semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone is a comma splice, and no punctuation is a run-on. A comma after \"and\" wrongly separates the conjunction from the clause it introduces."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.de9808140f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-4","passage":"<p>In 1905, Albert Einstein published four papers that transformed ______ one introduced the special theory of relativity, and another proposed that light consists of particles now called photons.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["physics","physics,","physics, which","physics:"],"answer":"D","explanation":"What follows describes the papers that transformed physics, and it's an independent clause, so a colon correctly introduces that explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. \"which\" makes \"one introduced the special theory of relativity...\" ungrammatical."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.47ee58b3c2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-5","passage":"<p>Author Toni Morrison's ______ which won the Pulitzer Prize in 1988, is based in part on the life of Margaret Garner, a woman who escaped slavery in 1856.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["novel Beloved,","novel Beloved","novel, Beloved,","novel, Beloved"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The title \"Beloved\" is essential, since Morrison wrote many novels, so no comma separates it from \"novel.\" The nonessential clause \"which won the Pulitzer Prize in 1988\" must begin with a comma after the title. Omitting that comma runs the clause into the title, and a comma before the title wrongly treats it as nonessential."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.9aefeec02f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-6","passage":"<p>Engineers designed the building's foundation to move slightly during ______ allowing the structure to absorb the energy of the shaking rather than crack.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["earthquakes:","earthquakes;","earthquakes,","earthquakes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"allowing the structure to absorb...\" is a participial phrase describing the result of the design, so a comma correctly attaches it to the main clause. A semicolon or period would leave the phrase as a fragment. A colon is not used to introduce a participial phrase like this."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.820c367012","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-7","passage":"<p>The museum's new exhibit features three ______ a carved canoe from Hawaii, a woven cloak from New Zealand, and a painted drum from Tahiti.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["objects, these are","objects:","objects;","objects, including:"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The list explains the \"three objects,\" and the colon follows a complete independent clause, so a colon correctly introduces it. A semicolon must be followed by an independent clause. \"including:\" puts a colon after a word that already introduces examples. \"these are\" creates a comma splice."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.7edaadde11","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-8","passage":"<p>Although the Rosetta Stone was discovered in ______ scholars did not fully decipher its hieroglyphs until 1822, when Jean-François Champollion published his findings.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["1799","1799:","1799,","1799;"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Although the Rosetta Stone was discovered in 1799\" is a dependent clause that comes before the main clause, so it is followed by a comma. No punctuation runs the clauses together. A semicolon or colon would leave the dependent clause standing alone as a fragment."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.452adbf5d4","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-9","passage":"<p>The ancient Greek ______ often called the \"father of history,\" traveled widely to gather accounts of the Persian Wars.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["historian, Herodotus","historian, Herodotus,","historian Herodotus,","historian Herodotus"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Herodotus\" identifies which historian, so it's essential and takes no comma before it. The phrase \"often called the 'father of history'\" is nonessential and needs a comma before it, which closes after \"history.\" So \"historian Herodotus,\" is correct. Leaving out the comma, or adding one before the name, is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.7095729195","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-10","passage":"<p>When the Panama Canal opened in 1914, it shortened the sea route between New York and San Francisco by about 8,000 ______ ships no longer had to sail around the southern tip of South America.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["miles","miles;","miles,","miles, since,"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Both parts are independent clauses, so a semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone is a comma splice. No punctuation is a run-on. \"since,\" with commas creates an incorrect interruption after the conjunction."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.3d1263d7a7","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-11","passage":"<p>Unlike most ______ the glass frog has a translucent belly through which its heart, liver, and digestive organs can be seen.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["frogs;","frogs,","frogs:","frogs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Unlike most frogs\" is an introductory phrase, so it takes a comma before the main clause. No comma makes the sentence hard to read (\"most frogs the glass frog\"). A semicolon or colon would separate the phrase from the clause it modifies."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.8ffb9ccf15","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-12","passage":"<p>The sculptor worked with an unusual combination of ______ reclaimed steel from demolished bridges, driftwood gathered along the Oregon coast, and glass from broken bottles.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["materials. Such as","materials, such as:","materials;","materials:"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A colon introduces the list that specifies the \"unusual combination of materials.\" A semicolon requires an independent clause after it. \"such as:\" places a colon directly after \"such as,\" which is incorrect. \"Such as\" after a period starts a fragment."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.a8e50276cf","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-13","passage":"<p>Hurricanes draw their energy from warm ocean ______ as a result, they typically weaken quickly after moving over land or cooler seas.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["water;","water, and,","water,","water"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Both parts are independent clauses, and \"as a result\" is not a conjunction, so a semicolon is required. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and \"and,\" with commas after it is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.3e3e80804e","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-14","passage":"<p>The linguist Ken Hale, who documented dozens of endangered languages in Australia, the Americas, and ______ believed that the loss of any language diminished humanity's understanding of the human mind.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["elsewhere—","elsewhere;","elsewhere","elsewhere,"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The nonessential clause \"who documented dozens of endangered languages...\" begins with a comma after \"Ken Hale,\" so it must end with a comma before the verb \"believed.\" No comma runs the clause into the verb. A semicolon separates the subject from its verb, and a dash doesn't match the opening comma."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.fd01ca3a1a","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-15","passage":"<p>The expedition's supply list was ______ each member was allotted a single wool blanket, a tin cup, and one pound of dried meat per day.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["spare:","spare","spare, where","spare,"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The second clause illustrates how spare the supply list was, and both parts are independent clauses, so a colon is correct. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and \"where\" doesn't make sense after \"spare.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.840a9a86f2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-16","passage":"<p>Mathematician Katherine Johnson, whose calculations helped guide the 1969 Apollo 11 mission to the ______ was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2015.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Moon—","Moon","Moon;","Moon,"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The nonessential clause \"whose calculations helped guide... to the Moon\" opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before \"was awarded.\" No comma runs it into the verb, a semicolon separates subject from verb, and a dash doesn't match the opening comma."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.a7321750db","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-17","passage":"<p>Many desert plants store water in thick, fleshy ______ others survive by sending roots deep underground to reach moisture far below the surface.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["stems the","stems, the","stems; the","stems, and, the"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The two independent clauses are correctly separated by a semicolon. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and \"and,\" with a comma after it is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.527288afe4","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-18","passage":"<p>The novel alternates between two ______ a young translator in 1950s Tokyo and her granddaughter, who discovers the translator's letters decades later.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["narrators;","narrators, being","narrators. One is","narrators:"],"answer":"D","explanation":"A colon introduces the explanation of who the \"two narrators\" are. A semicolon requires an independent clause, but what follows is a noun phrase. \"being\" creates an awkward, ungrammatical phrase. \"One is\" after a period produces a sentence (\"One is a young translator... and her granddaughter\") that lists two people after \"One is,\" which doesn't make sense."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.baae978e08","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"hard-BD","group":"hard-hard_data6-19","passage":"<p>Scientists at the observatory detected an unusual radio signal in ______ further analysis, however, revealed that it came from a microwave oven in the staff kitchen.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["2015,","2015","2015;","2015 and,"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Both parts are independent clauses, and \"however\" isn't a conjunction, so a semicolon is needed. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. Placing the comma after \"and\" instead of before it is incorrect."}
]);
